  "nameStory": "The 'hairy' in this plant's name comes from the soft, fuzzy coating of fine hairs that covers its stems and leaves, making it feel distinctly different from its smoother cousins. Like other St. John's-worts, it's named after St. John the Baptist because it traditionally blooms around his feast day (June 24th). The fuzzy texture is so noticeable that early botanists made it the defining feature in the plant's common name.",
  "description": "Hairy St. John's-wort is a medium-sized perennial wildflower that stands 1-3 feet tall with soft, fuzzy stems and leaves. It produces clusters of bright yellow, five-petaled flowers that are typical of the St. John's-wort family, each about half an inch across. The entire plant has a distinctly soft, velvety feel due to the fine hairs covering all parts. This European native is quite rare in New England, with only a couple of recorded sightings.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Texture",
      "description": "The entire plant feels noticeably soft and fuzzy to the touch, with fine hairs covering stems, leaves, and even flower buds - like touching velvet",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Flowers",
      "description": "Bright golden-yellow flowers with five petals, each about half an inch wide, arranged in loose clusters at the top of stems",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Leaves",
      "description": "Oval-shaped leaves arranged in opposite pairs along the stem, each leaf soft and fuzzy with visible veins",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Growth Habit",
      "description": "Upright perennial growing 1-3 feet tall with multiple branching stems forming a somewhat bushy appearance",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "This rare plant prefers disturbed soils, roadsides, and woodland edges in areas with partial shade to full sun. In its native Europe, it's found in grasslands and forest margins, so look for it in similar habitats in New England - old fields transitioning to woods or along rural road edges.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "New growth emerges as soft, fuzzy shoots that are immediately recognizable by their velvety texture.",
    "summer": "Peak blooming period with bright yellow flowers from June through August, making this the best time for identification.",
    "fall": "Flowers fade and seed capsules develop, while the fuzzy stems and leaves remain distinctive until frost.",
    "winter": "Dies back to ground level, leaving no above-ground parts to identify."
  },
  "funFact": "Despite being in the same family as the famous medicinal Common St. John's-wort, this fuzzy cousin is so rare in New England that finding one would be like discovering botanical treasure - there are more recorded sightings of some endangered orchids than this humble hairy herb!",
  "confusionNotes": "The soft, velvety texture immediately separates this from all other St. John's-worts in New England, which have smooth or only slightly hairy stems and leaves. Remember: if it feels like petting a fuzzy caterpillar, you've found the hairy one!",
  "contextualKnowledge": "This plant is native to Western Europe and is considered quite rare as an introduction to North America. Most St. John's-wort species people encounter will be the common smooth-stemmed varieties, making any fuzzy-textured find worth documenting and reporting to local botanists.",
  "compareHints": "Among New England's St. John's-worts, this is the only one with distinctly fuzzy, velvety stems and leaves - all others range from completely smooth to only slightly hairy. The Common St. John's-wort has smooth stems with raised ridges, while Spotted St. John's-wort has smooth leaves with translucent dots. If you can pet it like velvet, it's the hairy species.",
